9 Nondeterminism, approximation, and honest results
Not all computation is as clean as exact integers. For random simulations, optimization, parallel computation, and floating-point arithmetic, different bytes may comply with the contract. However, “the results are roughly the same” is not an acceptance rule.

9.1 Contracts for approximate output
The contract must specify:

the mathematical quantity being estimated;
its representation and units;
sources of error: rounding, discretization, sampling, termination, or others;
absolute/relative tolerances and cases in which either is meaningless;
the statistics or intervals being compared;
the number of repetitions and the aggregation rule; and
conditions that make the result inconclusive, rather than forcing a pass.

If the expected value is zero, relative error can be undefined or misleading. If a result lies near a threshold, choosing a tolerance after seeing the value biases the decision. Freeze the rule before execution and report the raw values together with the decision.


9.2 Trajectory repeatability is not distribution validation
Running a simulation twice with the same seed tests whether a particular trajectory can be repeated under particular conditions. Running many seeds tests empirical variation. Neither yet proves that the probabilistic model, generator, statistic, or interpretation is correct. The audit must address all four layers separately.

12 Checklist before accepting a result
Before calling a result reproducible, check that:

the claim, quantifiers, domain, and epistemic status are frozen;
code, inputs, configuration, oracle, and checker have byte identities;
runtime, dependencies, system, encoding, and sources of nondeterminism are recorded;
parameters, tolerances, arithmetic model, and seed/none are explicit;
another reader can follow the execution procedure and comparison rule;
at least two actual runs are recorded as separate events;
actual results are preserved even when they fail;
one negative control or mutation expected to fail has actually been tested;
the implementation is audited against the claim, not just the oracle;
universal proof is separated from finite checking;
a plain-text summary states the result, scope, and limitations; and
the provenance and rights of every component are stated.

